---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '~/navigation';

interface Props {
  id?: string;
}
const { id } = Astro.props;

const ORIGINAL_URL = 'https://github.com/rwardlow/pyMCU';
const RICHARD_URL = 'https://github.com/rwardlow';
const PYASTRA_URL = 'https://sourceforge.net/projects/pyastra/';
const CREDITS_URL = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/CREDITS.md`;

// Two columns, deliberately symmetrical: neither side is the "winner" here, so
// unlike the benchmark card on the landing page nothing is painted in accent
// except the links. The point is that these are different machines, not that
// one replaced the other.
const columns = [
  {
    year: '2012',
    kicker: 'The original',
    name: 'pyMCU',
    by: 'Richard Wardlow',
    byHref: RICHARD_URL,
    repo: 'rwardlow/pyMCU',
    repoHref: ORIGINAL_URL,
    rows: [
      { label: 'Python runs on', value: 'The host computer — PC, Mac or Linux' },
      { label: 'The microcontroller', value: 'Runs Richard’s firmware and takes commands over USB' },
      { label: 'How it works', value: 'A USB tether and a Python library on the other end of the cable' },
      {
        label: 'What it set out to do',
        value: 'Let anyone drive real electronics from Python, with no embedded expertise',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    year: '2026',
    kicker: 'This project',
    name: 'PyMCU',
    by: 'Iván Montiel Cardona',
    byHref: GITHUB_URL,
    repo: 'PyMCU/PyMCU',
    repoHref: GITHUB_URL,
    rows: [
      { label: 'Python runs on', value: 'The microcontroller itself' },
      { label: 'The microcontroller', value: 'Is the bare-metal target the code is compiled for' },
      { label: 'How it works', value: 'An ahead-of-time compiler — no board of its own, no runtime, no interpreter' },
      { label: 'What it set out to do', value: 'Make Python a first-class language for the chip itself' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<section {id} class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pb-12 sm:pb-16 lg:pb-20">
  <!-- Richard Wardlow: the reason this project is allowed to use the name. -->
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <p class="mb-2 text-base font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The name</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tighter text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Richard Wardlow and the original pyMCU
    </h2>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
      <p>
        In 2012, <a
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
          href={RICHARD_URL}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Richard Wardlow</a
        > built <a
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
          href={ORIGINAL_URL}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">pyMCU</a
        >: a small USB board around a PIC 16F1939, running firmware that spoke a clean protocol, paired with a Python
        library on the other end of the cable. You plugged it into a PC, Mac or Linux machine, wrote a few lines of
        Python, and an LED came on. Sensors, servos, real electronics — and no embedded expertise required. It was
        accessible, joyful and genuinely creative.
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="font-semibold text-default dark:text-slate-200">This project is not that project.</span> PyMCU today
        is an ahead-of-time compiler: it takes a statically-typed subset of Python and emits native machine code that runs
        on the microcontroller itself, with no host computer, no interpreter and no board of its own. The architecture is
        new, and the authorship and the mistakes are mine. What carried over is the name, and the conviction underneath it.
      </p>
      <!--
        First person from here on. This is a one-person project and the page is
        signed at the bottom, so narrating my own email in the third person read
        like a press office describing someone else.
      -->
      <p>
        In March 2026 I wrote to Richard to ask whether this project could carry the name forward. He said yes —
        immediately, warmly, and without conditions.
      </p>
      <!--
        His reply is not quoted here, and that is deliberate. He gave permission
        for the name; he did not offer his words for a marketing page, and asking
        him for them afterwards would turn a generous answer into a transaction.
        That he said yes is mine to report. How he said it is his.
      -->
      <p>
        He did not have to do that. He owed this project nothing, and he answered a stranger’s email by giving away the
        name of something he had made. That deserves to be said in public and by name, which is what this page is for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Same card treatment as the firmware-size benchmark on the landing page, so
       the comparison reads as part of the site rather than a bolted-on table. -->
  <h3 class="mt-14 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
    Same name, two different machines
  </h3>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted dark:text-gray-400">
    Neither approach replaced the other. They answer the same question — can Python reach real hardware? — from opposite
    ends of the USB cable.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-gray-900 dark:bg-gray-950 ring-1 ring-white/10 shadow-xl p-6 sm:p-10">
    <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-0 md:divide-x md:divide-white/10">
      {
        columns.map((col, index) => (
          <div class:list={[index === 0 ? 'md:pr-10' : 'md:pl-10']}>
            <p class="font-mono text-4xl tabular-nums text-gray-600">{col.year}</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500">{col.kicker}</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-semibold text-white">{col.name}</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">
              by{' '}
              <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={col.byHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {col.by}
              </a>
            </p>

            <dl class="mt-7 space-y-5">
              {col.rows.map((row) => (
                <div>
                  <dt class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500">{row.label}</dt>
                  <dd class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-200">{row.value}</dd>
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>

            <a
              class="mt-7 inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs text-gray-400 hover:text-accent"
              href={col.repoHref}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              <Icon name="tabler:brand-github" class="h-4 w-4" />
              {col.repo}
            </a>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="mt-9 border-t border-white/10 pt-6 text-xs text-gray-500">
      Two very different approaches to one shared mission: make microcontrollers reachable through Python.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Pyastra: owed the premise, not the name. Kept deliberately quieter. -->
  <div class="mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="mb-2 text-base font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The idea</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tighter text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Pyastra got there first
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
      <p>
        Six years before either pyMCU, an open-source project on SourceForge called <a
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
          href={PYASTRA_URL}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Pyastra</a
        > — PYthon to ASsembler TRAnslator — was already compiling a subset of Python into Microchip PIC16 assembly. It was
        written in Python, released under the GNU GPL v2, and it never got past alpha; the last release was around 2006,
        years before MicroPython, CircuitPython or any of today’s Python-on-hardware ecosystems existed.
      </p>
      <p>
        It proved the idea was not a dream. Compiling Python instead of interpreting it was possible on a
        microcontroller, and someone had demonstrated it two decades ago. This compiler owes Pyastra the premise.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-slate-800 pt-8">
    <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Thank you, Richard. The name is in good hands.</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-widest text-muted dark:text-gray-400">Iván Montiel Cardona</p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-muted dark:text-gray-400">
      The same credits ship with the compiler, in <a
        class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
        href={CREDITS_URL}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">CREDITS.md</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
